"<span>Blind: hide the heating curve and the energy split</span>",
"Atoms view: {#} argon atoms that attract each other at a distance and repel when they touch (the Lennard-Jones potential, with argon's bond strength ε/k = {#} K and size σ = {#} nm, cut off at {#} nm). Newton's second law moves every atom in steps of {#} fs. The sample repeats sideways, so an atom leaving one side comes back in at the other, as if it were part of a wide sheet. A free piston presses down with the pressure you set, and the heater adds energy to the atoms touching the floor. The piston is idealized: it pushes with exactly the set pressure (its gauge shows it), with no weights, from {#} to {#} atm. At low pressure the vapor needs far more room, so the picture squeezes heights above {#} nm (a dashed line marks where) to keep the piston in view; the Column height readout is the true height. The column stops at {#} nm, so at {#} atm the vapor fills it and then the volume stays fixed. Temperature is the average kinetic energy of the atoms (KE = {#}/{#} kT per atom); the bond energy U is their potential energy, negative because bound atoms have less energy than separated ones. The heat added equals the change in KE + U + PV: the first law at constant pressure. The run lasts nanoseconds, so the heater is far stronger than a stove. Two things move the temperatures away from real argon's {#} K (melting) and {#} K (boiling at {#} atm). The setups use {#} atm, not {#} atm, so that the vapor stays near the sample (at {#} atm it would need more than ten times the room), and a liquid boils hotter at a higher pressure: that is why boiling here comes well above {#} K. And the sample is only a few nanometers thick, with many atoms at a surface, and each atom feels only the atoms within {#} nm, so it is held together less firmly than a flask of argon: it melts below {#} K and its latent heats are smaller. The shape of the heating curve, and where the heat goes, are the same. A small sample can also overheat for a moment before the first vapor forms, then settle back to the boiling plateau; real liquids do this too (superheating). Temperatures and energies on screen are averages over the last {#} ns. The flat view shows a slice {#} nm thick through the middle of the sample, with the atoms behind it faint; the 3D view shows them all.",
"Beaker view: a sample heated at a constant power P in a perfectly insulated beaker open to the air at {#} atm, so all of the heat goes into the sample (none into the glass or the room) and the vapor leaves. Specific heats and latent heats are from OpenStax University Physics Volume {#}, Tables {#} and {#}: ice {#} J/kg·°C, water {#}, ethanol {#}; water melts at {#} °C with Lf = {#} kJ/kg and boils at {#} °C with Lv = {#} kJ/kg; ethanol boils at {#} °C with Lv = {#} kJ/kg. Each stage takes heat Q = mcΔT or Q = mL, delivered at Q = Pt. Evaporation below the boiling point is neglected. The balance is tared, so it reads the sample only. The covered beaker beside it is the same sample on the same hot plate with a lid: until it boils the two match, then its vapor condenses on the lid and runs back, so nothing leaves and its balance keeps reading the whole mass (mass is conserved; only the open beaker loses the vapor). The lid passes the heat on to the room, which is not modeled, so the covered sample stays at the boiling point. Ice is drawn as cubes that shrink as they melt, their total volume following the mass of ice left.",
"Phase diagram view: each line is where two phases can sit together, drawn from the international reference equations for the measured boundaries. Water: the vapor pressure of the liquid from W. Wagner and A. Pruss ({#}), and the sublimation and melting (ordinary ice, ice Ih) pressures from W. Wagner, T. Riethmann, R. Feistel and A. H. Harvey ({#}), both adopted by IAPWS; triple point {#} K and {#} Pa, critical point {#} K and {#} MPa. Ice Ih melts down to {#} K at {#} MPa, where ice III takes over; the denser ices at higher pressures are left out, and the diagram stops at {#} MPa. Carbon dioxide: R. Span and W. Wagner ({#}); triple point {#} K and {#} MPa, critical point {#} K and {#} MPa. On these scales water boils at {#} °C at {#} atm ({#} K); textbooks round it to {#} °C, as the Beaker view does. The pressure axis is logarithmic: each grid line is ten times the one below. Play heats (or cools) the sample at the dot's pressure: T moves at a steady rate between the lines and holds for {#} s at each line while the phase changes; the hold stands for the latent heat, and its length is not to scale. The molecule sketch beside the diagram is a picture of the arrangement, not a simulation; the Atoms view simulates argon atoms through the same changes. Pressure presets: the top of Everest ({#} m) in the US Standard Atmosphere {#}, {#} kPa; a pressure cooker held {#} atm above sea level air, {#} kPa.",
"Vapor view: water in a sealed jar of air, or in an open pot. The saturation vapor pressure of water, p_sat(T), is from W. Wagner and A. Pruss ({#}), adopted by IAPWS ({#} kPa at {#} °C; {#} kPa at {#} °C). The vapor is an ideal gas, so its density is ρ = pM/(RT) with M = {#} g/mol. Molecules leave the water at a rate set by its temperature and come back at a rate in proportion to the vapor pressure, so p rises toward p_sat as {#} − e^(−t/{#} s); the time constant is chosen so the jar saturates in seconds, where a real jar takes minutes to hours because the vapor has to diffuse through the air. Relative humidity is p/p_sat(T); the dew point is the temperature at which p_sat equals p. Cooled below it, the vapor above p_sat condenses at once on the glass as dew (in a real jar some fog can form first), so the relative humidity stays at {#} %. The thermostat moves the temperature of the water and the air together, at most {#} °C per second. In the open pot the water boils when its vapor pressure reaches the outside pressure, and the temperature then stays at that boiling point while the heat boils the water away. The sealed jar keeps the air it was closed with, so the pressure inside is the air's pressure (in proportion to the kelvin temperature) plus the vapor's, always above p_sat: it never boils. Outside pressures for Denver ({#} m) and the top of Everest ({#} m) are from the US Standard Atmosphere {#} ({#} and {#} kPa); a pressure cooker holds {#} atm above sea level air, {#} kPa. The arrows at the water's surface are in proportion to the rates at which molecules leave (p_sat) and return (p). Each drawn vapor molecule stands for {#} g/m³ of vapor, at most {#} drawn.",
